  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Planning the Framework**\n\nI\'m focusing on meticulous design before I write any code. I\'ve started by reviewing the verification plan, as required. My immediate focus is on the architecture and visual direction, specifically for a mobile-first application that handles emergency motorbike rescue and 24/7 safe station location services, tentatively named \"\304\220\303\252m An To\303\240n\" or \"MotoSOS.\"\n\n\n**Defining the Visuals**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the visual design for \"\304\220\303\252m An To\303\240n.\" I\'m prioritizing a mobile-first approach, especially the Bottom Sheet design for easy thumb operation, splitting the screen in both mobile and desktop views to maintain functionality. The mobile layout splits vertically, and the desktop view provides a full-bleed map experience. The key goal is to avoid the trapped phone card look on larger screens.\n\n\n**Refining the Interface**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the UI, specifically the layout. I\'m focusing on the responsive design for both mobile and desktop views, with a focus on one-handed use on mobile. The application will feature a clear split: an interactive map and a bottom sheet, rounded on mobile, and with an added vertical split and border for the desktop experience. The key here is to add the \"Demo: Gi\341\272\243 l\341\272\255p th\341\273\243 nh\341\272\255n \304\221\306\241n\" button to test the mechanic state transitions. I\'ll place it in the top-right corner, ensuring clear visibility and clickability, which can be active in tracking mode. I should also implement clear state advancements, such as \"Searching,\" \"Accepted,\" \"Arriving,\" and \"Completed,\" to allow clear state changes during testing.\n\n\n**Implementing State Transitions**\n\nI\'ve been working on the mechanic state transitions for the UI demo. My goal is to make the \"Demo: Gi\341\272\243 l\341\272\255p th\341\273\243 nh\341\272\255n \304\221\306\241n\" button more dynamic and intuitive. I\'m focusing on making it context-aware; it will now update its label based on the current state. When clicked, I\'m ensuring it seamlessly advances through the states, transitioning from \"Searching\" to \"Accepted,\" then \"Arriving,\" and finally \"Completed,\" in any state. If pressed at any time, it\'ll fast-forward the state to either Step 3 or a new mechanic dispatch.\n\n\n**Specifying the Visual Style**\n\nI\'ve been defining the visual style rules and typefaces. I want to avoid specific fonts, and will use \"Plus Jakarta Sans\" for display and body text, and \"JetBrains Mono\" for tabular numerals. I have a 60-30-10 Dark Mode palette. I want the canvas to be a dark slate, the surfaces to be darker, and accents to be amber/orange for urgent calls and emerald for safe stations.\n\n\n**Refining Interaction Clarity**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the interaction clarity. I want to pair mechanic arrival indicators with explicit text labels and icons, avoiding hue-only signaling. I\'m instituting anti-slop disciplines like no decorative pulsing dots or static pill enclosures. My approach will be clean unboxed text for metadata, minimum 44px touch targets, and smooth transitions using `AnimatePresence`. Finally, I\'ll incorporate loading skeleton/spinner states during transitions.\n\n\n**Detailing the Rescue Flow**\n\nI\'m now detailing the step-by-step rescue order flow. I\'m focusing on user journey clarity. I\'ve mapped out the steps from emergency call initiation to safe station selection. I\'m prioritizing concise previews for safe stations with walking distances when users click station pins. The bottom sheet will show the primary CTA \"G\341\273\214I S\341\273\254A XE KH\341\272\250N C\341\272\244P\".\n\n\n**Mapping the Rescue Journey**\n\nI\'m now detailing the user journey after safe station selection. Choosing a station updates the pickup address and displays the route. Pressing \"X\303\241c nh\341\272\255n g\341\273\215i c\341\273\251u h\341\273\231\" triggers a loading state and Step 3, with an animated radar. Then, clicking the demo mechanic button shows mechanic details, ETA, and live tracking. Finally, clicking the demo button again transitions to the completion state with a rescue summary.\n\n\n**Structuring the Files**\n\nI\'m structuring the rescue app\'s modular file setup, keeping each under 250 lines. Types are in `rescue.ts`, mock data in `mockRescueData.ts`, and core logic in `rescueLogic.ts`. The state machine handles transitions and data computations. I\'m focusing on rescue flow and state transitions, and the UI will reflect each state, from idle to rescue summary, with clear options and concise details.\n\n\n**Implementing Core Features**\n\nI\'m now implementing the core components. I\'ve been working on the implementation, including unit tests and building the UI elements. Specifically, I\'m happy to announce the implementation of the map, overlay, bottom sheet, and various steps of functionality, each with detailed design and functionality. I\'m focusing on responsiveness and smooth transitions. I also made a mock test for mechanic tracking.\n\n\n**Defining Component Breakdown**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a defined file and component breakdown. I\'ve broken the components into steps, focusing on a clear rescue flow. Step 1 focuses on issue selection, Step 2 is pickup location, and Step 3 is live tracking. I\'ll orchestrate the states, as well as ensure loading state and transitions are smooth. Next, I\'ll update the index and set the fonts.\n\n\n"
