  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Requirements Diligently**\n\nI\'ve successfully validated all eight unit tests. Now, my focus shifts towards crafting modular UI components, each under the 250-line limit, as per the specified design principles and mobile application guidelines. I\'m carefully reviewing all requirements to ensure I haven\'t overlooked anything.\n\n\n**Designing Mobile Interface**\n\nI am now focusing on the mobile interface design. I\'m prioritizing a user-friendly layout with a dark mode theme (`slate-950`). The interactive simulated map will be positioned in the top half of the screen on mobile devices, with a tactile Bottom Sheet occupying the bottom half. On desktop, a split-view layout is being considered, ensuring a clean and efficient display.\n\n\n**Developing Order Flow**\n\nI\'m currently immersed in developing the step-by-step order flow within the Bottom Sheet, focusing on clarity and ease of use. Step 1 (`IssueSelectionStep`) is now functioning: users can swiftly select an issue from predefined options or specify a custom one with an optional short note. I\'m prioritizing accessible touch targets ($\\ge 44\\text{px}$). I\'m also ensuring the second step (`PickupSelectionStep`) allows users to choose between waiting at their current location or moving to the nearest safe station, displaying station options with walking distance clearly on both the map and the preview card.\n\n\n**Implementing Tracking Updates**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in implementing the tracking step (`TrackingStep`) for the rescue order flow. I\'ve designed smooth loading state transitions (around 450ms) before advancing to Step 3. The `searching` state presents a \"Finding a rescuer near you...\" message, with a radar animation and cancel button. The `accepted` state shows the rescuer\'s details (name, rating, vehicle, ETA), call and locate buttons, and visualizes their route on the map. I\'ve also incorporated a demo button in the top corner that allows me to immediately simulate an accepted order.\n\n\n**Refining Transition Logic**\n\nI\'m currently refining the state transition logic, specifically focusing on the \"accepted\" state within the tracking flow. My goal is a smooth transition to \"completed\" upon a click, with the correct button labels reflecting the state change. I will place a demo button in the top-right corner to allow the user to easily simulate the order progression. I\'m also ensuring the test transitions from any state and step! I will make sure there are no static pill boxes.\n\n\n"
