  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Verifying The Context**\n\nI\'ve just finished verifying that both `ultra-mode`, `frontend-design`, and `computer-use` are active and available within this environment. Now, I\'m carefully reviewing the prompt to ensure a solid grasp of the task. I need to make sure I understand everything.\n\n\n**Analyzing Environment Setup**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the environment setup. I need to make sure I understand how to deal with the key. I see I must handle client-side environment variables correctly, especially given the React + Vite setup. I\'m noting the importance of `VITE_` prefixes and the need for a fallback mechanism to prevent errors. I\'m also reviewing `.env.example` to understand how environment variables are defined and the importance of proxy routes for API keys.\n\n\n**Confirming Dependencies and Imports**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the dependency structure, specifically focusing on `maplibre-gl`. I\'ve confirmed we need it, along with its CSS file, and I\'ve noted the cartoDB style URL. I plan to install it using the `install_applet_package` tool. I\'m also ensuring that the TypeScript types for `maplibre-gl` are correctly imported and utilized.\n\n\n**Designing The Bottom Sheet**\n\nI\'m now completely focused on the bottom sheet\'s design, especially the layout. The user wants the map full-screen *behind* the sheet on mobile. I\'m noting the need to hide the default MapLibre controls, but keep a single \"Return to my location\" button. Also, `map.fitBounds` is important, with padding to ensure the route fits above the bottom sheet. I\'m focusing on the responsive layout using classes.\n\n\n**Refining Button Placement**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on positioning the \"V\341\273\201 v\341\273\213 tr\303\255 c\341\273\247a t\303\264i\" button. I\'m focusing on its placement relative to the bottom sheet on both mobile and desktop. I\'m carefully considering how to handle the different screen sizes, which will require distinct positioning and padding configurations to maintain the desired floating effect. The `fitBounds` adjustments for different device sizes have become very important.\n\n\n**Optimizing UX with GPS**\n\nI\'m now implementing the smooth UX for GPS. I\'ve designed a system for immediate fallback to the HCMC center coordinates upon permission denial or errors from `navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition`. The map initializes with the fallback center, preventing a blank screen. If real GPS data becomes available, the map smoothly updates with `map.flyTo`, ensuring a clean transition. I\'ve also incorporated a timeout for the GPS call, along with `enableHighAccuracy: true`.\n\n\n**Implementing Geoapify API**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in integrating the Geoapify Places API. I\'ve designed the URL structure, incorporating dynamic longitude and latitude parameters, plus the API key. Now, the main thing is handling the potential errors when fetching. When the `apiKey` is present, I\'ll need to parse the GeoJSON `features`, mapping them into `SafeStation` objects. I\'ve mapped each feature with unique id, name, category and address. I\'m focusing on handling different response scenarios gracefully.\n\n\n\n**Calculating Walking Distances**\n\nI\'m now calculating walking distances and times to each station. I\'ll utilize the provided `feature.properties.distance`, or I\'ll compute the Haversine distance in meters if needed. I\'m carefully converting meters to walking minutes, ensuring a minimum of one minute. I\'m also planning the integration of mock safe stations with real coordinates as a fallback. I\'ll maintain real coordinates for the fallback stations to make sure markers and routing will function, even without the Geoapify API key.\n\n\n**Refining Route Handling**\n\nI\'m now refining route handling, and I\'m very happy. The process STILL uses the real OSRM API, drawing real walking routes on the map! And I\'ve implemented a graceful fallback to a straight GeoJSON `LineString` route if OSRM is unreachable, ensuring a clean experience.\n\nI\'m now also focused on custom markers and route layers. I\'m excited about the design - the user marker is a blue dot with a radar ping, and safe station markers are emerald with walking badges. Clicking a station marker selects it, triggers the OSRM route if in Step 2, and adjusts `fitBounds`. I\'m also preparing an amber mechanic marker.\n\n\n**Designing Route Layers**\n\nI\'m now carefully designing the route layers for clarity and aesthetics, and ensuring a smooth user experience. I\'m focusing on a GeoJSON source called `\'walking-route-source\'`, and I\'ve designed a green line layer. The plan includes a subtle loading indicator, and I\'ll clear the route cleanly upon switching states, which is quite important to keep the map clean. I\'m also ensuring the correct fitBounds calculations, based on the current context.\n\n\n\n**Positioning \"V\341\273\201 v\341\273\213 tr\303\255\"**\n\nI\'m now implementing the floating \"V\341\273\201 v\341\273\213 tr\303\255 c\341\273\247a t\303\264i\" button. I\'ve successfully placed the button (`LocateFixed` icon, `aria-label=\"V\341\273\201 v\341\273\213 tr\303\255 c\341\273\247a t\303\264i\"`) at the bottom right corner, above the bottom sheet\'s edge. Clicking triggers a smooth `map.flyTo` animation, returning to the user\'s location.\n\n"
