  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Observing The Visuals**\n\nI\'m incredibly excited about the real Dark Matter map combined with the OSRM walking route! The visuals are striking. I\'m also really glad we caught that mobile bug, as it highlights the value of these visual comparisons. This progress is fantastic.\n\n\n**Analyzing The MapLibre Bug**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed a critical mobile rendering issue. The map wasn\'t fitting within the canvas due to excessive padding calculations combined with the BottomSheet\'s height. Specifically, on a `390x844` mobile screen, the BottomSheet occupies a significant portion, leaving limited space at the top. The `fitBounds` and `flyTo` methods\' padding caused the user marker and stations to appear behind the Bottom Sheet. I\'m investigating how MapLibre\'s `flyTo` method shifts the visual center when `padding.bottom` is used, and why this results in markers being hidden.\n\n\n**Investigating The Center**\n\nI\'m now diving into the centering issue. Initializing with the user\'s location works, but then an immediate `useEffect` with `flyTo` is triggered, leading to a visual shift. I\'m focusing on why this `flyTo` action, triggered on mount, offsets the map\'s initial center, particularly given the map\'s warnings about fitting within the canvas due to bounds and padding. The problem seems to be happening when the map initially loads. The map loads and then immediately flies to the location, resulting in the user\'s location being hidden.\n\n\n**Examining The Camera**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on why the camera shifted the center northward. I noticed that when the padding bottom is 300px, MapLibre shifts the center *upward* on the screen. This means points south of the center are pushed down. I\'m focusing on why the `flyTo` method is moving the camera too far north. Line 121: `map.flyTo({ center: [lng, lat], padding: { top: 40, bottom: 300 } })` may be the root cause. This northward push has critical consequences.\n\n\n**Revisiting Camera Positioning**\n\nI\'m now tracing the root cause of the camera offset more concretely. I have a hunch that the `flyTo` method was called on initial render before `map.on(\'load\')` or while the map was initializing! I now have more information. I\'m taking a close look at that line in `RescueMap.tsx`. I believe the initial flyTo is the key to understanding this camera\'s strange behavior! I am comparing the behavior on load.\n\n\n**Questioning The Padding**\n\nI\'m now deeply puzzled by the padding calculation issue. Specifically, on a `390x844` mobile canvas, with `padding: { top: 80, bottom: 380, left: 40, right: 40 }`, MapLibre warns that it \"cannot fit within canvas\". I need to understand why. The canvas size (`clientHeight = 844`) seems correct based on the container hierarchy, so I\'m investigating the sequence of events and the `fitBounds` call. I\'ll re-examine the test script steps for clues.\n\n\n**Unraveling The Conflict**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical padding conflict. It seems `flyTo` *persists* padding, which then *conflicts* with later `fitBounds` calls, leading to the \"map cannot fit within canvas\" error. The persistent `flyTo` padding is not overridden when calling `fitBounds`. I\'m going to look at how MapLibreGL handles padding persistence. Also, I\'m focusing on why the viewport size is not properly set!\n\n\n**Investigating The Coordinates**\n\nI\'m now investigating a discrepancy in marker coordinates between screenshots. I\'m puzzled why \"Circle K\" is at `y ~ 360px` in `r4-mobile-idle.png`, even though it should be near the blue dot. The coordinates suggest a northward shift of 145 meters, but the visual shift is much greater. This could be padding or zooming related. I am also investigating the impact of viewport setting.\n\n\n"
