  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Planning Codebase Upgrades**\n\nI\'m struck by the codebase\'s present cleanliness! I\'m now meticulously planning the upcoming upgrades and modular architecture. My goal is to maintain readability, ensuring every file remains under 250 lines and adheres to ultra-mode and frontend-design standards. I\'m moving on to review each of the three requirements with great detail.\n\n\n**Refining Price Accuracy**\n\nI\'m now implementing exact, fixed prices in Step 1, specifically within `IssueSelectionStep.tsx` and `mockRescueData.ts`. The previously estimated price ranges are being replaced with clear, all-inclusive prices: for flat tires, it\'s 85,000 VND; dead engines, 120,000 VND; out of fuel, 80,000 VND; battery jumps, 90,000 VND; and other issues, a diagnostic fee of 50,000 VND. Each includes a detailed subtitle for clarity.\n\n\n**Analyzing UI Updates**\n\nI\'m now examining `HomeActionGrid.tsx` to confirm that changes to labels, specifically `\'Kh\303\241c\'` to `\'S\341\273\261 c\341\273\221 kh\303\241c\'`, don\'t disrupt the UI or icon alignment. I\'m also reviewing `formatVnd(amount: number)` and specifying the format as `85.000\304\221` with `tabular-nums` and `font-sans` for clear, aligned numbers. I\'m focusing on Step 2, `PickupSelectionStep.tsx`, or an extracted `InvoiceSummaryCard`, to build a transparent invoice with service price, discounts, and the final total in bold. Also in Step 2, I\'ll update the final amount in the button text.\n\n\n**Implementing Discount Selection**\n\nI\'m now integrating a discount selection feature, similar to Xanh SM, in Step 2. I\'m adding a compact \"\306\257u \304\221\303\243i\" (Offers) bar above the confirmation button. This will trigger a modal/drawer that slides up when tapped, ensuring the bottom sheet remains spacious. I\'m carefully considering how to manage the bottom sheet\'s height in `BottomSheetShell.tsx` to prevent any content from being obscured. Also, I\'m working on strikethrough original prices, and a final bold price, displayed in the invoice and confirm button.\n\n\n**Designing Voucher Functionality**\n\nI\'m now implementing the voucher selection process using `VoucherDrawerModal.tsx`. I\'m employing `AnimatePresence` for a smooth slide-up animation. I\'m focusing on showcasing three available vouchers, offering single-tap application. I\'m designing a system that handles specific voucher requirements like `ANTOAN20`, which requires \'safe_station\' pickup mode. If the pickup mode doesn\'t match, the \"Apply\" button will be disabled with a clear prompt. I\'m carefully managing the logic to invalidate any discount if the mode is changed.\n\n\n**Enhancing Voucher System**\n\nI\'m now implementing automatic voucher de-selection if `ANTOAN20` is selected and the location changes, enforcing this invariant in the pricing logic. I\'ve defined new vouchers, including `DEMKHUYA15` and `BANMOI25`. I\'m also adding a toggle to remove selected vouchers within the drawer. I\'m focusing on displaying applied voucher badges. My plan includes correctly handling discount calculations and displaying crossed-out original prices, along with the updated total.\n\n\n**Mapping Mechanic\'s Route**\n\nI\'m now carefully planning the implementation of the mechanic\'s route on the map, focusing on the mechanic\'s starting location being roughly 1.3km from the pickup point after user confirmation. I\'m calculating coordinate differences using Haversine distances for accuracy, then I will implement the calls to the OSRM Driving API to render the correct path on the map. I\'ll include a unit test to verify the distance calculation.\n\n\n**Defining Route Display**\n\nI\'m now writing `fetchMechanicDrivingRoute` in `src/utils/mapApi.ts`, calling the OSRM endpoint with a fallback polyline for network errors. This returns route geometry, distance, and duration. For the map, I\'ll update `\'mechanic-route-line\'` with an amber color and solid 4px line.\n\n\n**Visualizing Route Details**\n\nI\'m now focusing on visually refining the mechanic\'s route. I\'m incorporating an amber route line with a subtle white casing for visibility on the map. Upon loading, I\'ll update the source geometry with OSRM `LineString` and fit map bounds. I\'ll make a helper to sample coordinates for a smooth animation along the route.\n\n\n**Updating Mechanic Visualization**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a smooth mechanic visualization! I\'ll update the mechanic\'s location with each tick, using OSRM route coordinates and a dedicated motorbike marker in `mapMarkers.ts`. I am adding a live ETA badge to the marker, as well as a countdown. At the destination, I\'ll switch to \'\304\220\303\243 t\341\273\233i \304\221i\341\273\203m h\341\272\271n\' text. I\'ll also render a new mechanic info card with a call and message feature.\n\n\n\n**Refining Action Button**\n\nI\'m now implementing two large action buttons in the mechanic info card, styled side-by-side with a minimum height of 46px. The \"G\341\273\215i \304\221i\341\273\207n\" button uses an HTML link to trigger a native phone call, displaying an in-app status banner. Also, I\'m setting a constant `MOCK_MECHANIC.phone` to `0909 888 999`, so the phone number matches consistently! The \"Nh\341\272\257n tin\" button will open a chat drawer for quick communication, as well.\n\n\n"
