  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Request Thoroughly**\n\nI\'m meticulously dissecting the request, ensuring I haven\'t missed a single nuance. The key skills have been noted; ultra-mode, frontend design, and computer use are all in my context now. I\'m focusing on identifying every specific aspect so that I have a holistic view of the issue.\n\n\n**Defining the Scope Precisely**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the specific files I need to examine for line-accurate context, and have listed them to facilitate the design process. Specifically, I\'m verifying the exact contents of hooks and utility files, since they form the core of my recent work in this session. Next, I will begin to design the precise mathematical and architectural components of the solution.\n\n\n**Calculating the Initial Offset**\n\nI\'ve precisely calculated the initial offset for the mechanic\'s starting point from the pickup coordinate. I\'m focusing on ensuring the distance falls within the 700-900m range and avoids river crossings, specifically in District 1 of Ho Chi Minh City. I\'ll need to update `buildMechanicApproachCoord` in `rescueLogic.ts` and the unit test in `rescueLogic.test.ts` with these new coordinates.\n\n\n**Refining the Anchor Point**\n\nI\'m now deeply engaged with the `Marker` component in MapLibre, specifically focusing on the `anchor` property and its interaction with the mechanic\'s visual representation. I realized that the current anchor point (`bottom`) is misaligning the icon with the yellow route line. To address this, I\'m considering using `center` or structuring the icon for proper alignment, thus meeting Requirement 3 accurately. I\'m also confirming SRM\'s coordinate snapping behavior and the sampling technique\'s impact on uniform route steps.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Center Precisely**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial discovery: changing the wrapper to `relative flex h-10 w-10 items-center justify-center`, with an `anchor: \'center\'` on the marker, solves a significant visual misalignment. This pins the motorbike icon\'s center precisely to the start of the yellow line at `[currentPos.lng, currentPos.lat]`. This resolves a key visual issue.\n\n**Designing Smooth Segment Interpolation**\n\nMy focus has shifted to designing a pure math helper function. The goal is to perform segment-by-segment distance-based interpolation, allowing deterministic unit testing. I will make sure that it runs smoothly in `requestAnimationFrame` at 60fps without causing unnecessary React re-renders, while following `ultra-mode` best practices.\n\n\n**Optimizing for Performance**\n\nI\'m now separating pure geometry and progress math into a dedicated file, facilitating deterministic unit testing. I\'m focusing on `buildRouteCumulativeProfile` to precompute distances for each segment, ensuring that even with varying OSRM vertices, segments are weighted by Haversine length. `sampleRouteAtProgress` will then utilize binary search to find an accurate interpolated location based on a given progress value, addressing potential unnecessary re-renders in React with these functions.\n\n\n**Implementing the Overlay Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing the exact logic for precise coordinate interpolation to build remaining GeoJSON. It correctly calculates the current position and remaining coordinates. I\'m focusing on ensuring perfect sync with the animation for a 60fps refresh rate inside a `requestAnimationFrame` loop without causing redundant React re-renders. I am specifically considering how to manage ETA and status updates with the animated Mechanic overlay.\n\n\n**Finalizing the Mechanic Overlay**\n\nI\'m now implementing the `useRescueMapMechanicOverlay` hook to manage the mechanic\'s overlay. I\'m focusing on the logic: when tracking is accepted and route coordinates are available, I build the profile, lock the marker to the initial coordinate, and render the route. A `requestAnimationFrame` loop handles smooth animation at 60fps, avoiding React re-renders. The `currentPos` and remaining route are updated on each frame.\n\n\n**Refining the ETA Calculations**\n\nI\'m now focusing on calculating a remaining ETA, considering all aspects. I\'ve calculated the minutes remaining based on `remainingFraction` and updated the mechanic\'s badge appropriately. I now only notify React when `etaMinutes` changes to minimize updates, specifically avoiding rapid updates and reducing to 5 React updates in 50 seconds. I\'m also ensuring the React update occurs when `progress >= 1`, in addition to `etaMinutes` changes, in preparation for the arrival.\n\n\n**Determining State Transitions**\n\nI\'m now examining how the app handles `trackingStatus` and its transitions, specifically considering the scenario where the mechanic arrives. I\'m reviewing `TrackingStep.tsx`, `AcceptedMechanicCard.tsx`, `TopOverlayBar.tsx`, and `ActivityTab.tsx` to understand the current behavior of `\'completed\'` and how to introduce a new state like `\'arrived\'`. The goal is to clearly display \"Th\341\273\243 \304\221\303\243 t\341\273\233i n\306\241i\" and allow interaction with mechanic info even after arrival, and to remove the yellow route appropriately, as the current completion message seems more final than desired.\n\n\n**Defining New Arrival Logic**\n\nI\'m now integrating new arrival logic, incorporating both a visual and functional update. I\'m focusing on ensuring `\"Xong ca\"`/`\"Ho\303\240n t\341\272\245t c\341\273\251u h\341\273\231\"` completes the rescue (as before), and also on the transition to the arrival state. When the progress is complete, `onMechanicArrived()` transitions the tracking status to `\'arrived\'`. I\'m updating `AcceptedMechanicCard` to display \"Th\341\273\243 \304\221\303\243 t\341\273\233i n\306\241i\", using the existing structure and making new visual updates. Specifically, I am displaying \"Th\341\273\243 \304\221\303\243 t\341\273\233i n\306\241i \302\267 S\341\272\265n s\303\240ng h\341\273\227 tr\341\273\243\" as the main heading, and \"\304\220\303\243 t\341\273\233i n\306\241i\" as a green pill. I\'m also preparing to remove the yellow route, and ensure the current unit tests pass.\n\n\n**Verifying Final State Handling**\n\nI\'m now meticulously verifying that all states, including the new \'arrived\' state, are handled correctly across the application. I\'m double-checking the visual changes: the map marker badge reads `\"Th\341\273\243 Tu\341\272\245n \302\267 \304\220\303\243 t\341\273\233i n\306\241i\"`. The user still has the \"G\341\273\215i \304\221i\341\273\207n\" and \"Nh\341\272\257n tin\" options. I\'m confirming that the \"Ho\303\240n t\341\272\245t c\341\273\251u h\341\273\231\" button (or the \"Xong ca\" button) triggers a move to `\'completed\'`. I\'ve verified that the UI updates for the \'arrived\' state in `ActivityTab.tsx` and `TopOverlayBar.tsx` are correct. Now I\'m preparing to handle edge cases, such as temporary tab switches, to maintain the animation\'s smooth function.\n\n\n**Refining Marker Placement**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the precise initial placement of the mechanic marker. It\'s clear that I should *not* place the marker at the raw starting coordinate before OSRM data loads. Instead, I need to wait for the `mechanicRoute` data to arrive and then place the marker at the very first coordinate in that route. This ensures the alignment requirement, preventing initial marker drift, and maintaining smooth animation, even across tab switches. I am now incorporating this logic into the marker\'s initialization process.\n\n\n**Confirming Starting Coordinate**\n\nI\'m now confirming the starting coordinate logic is flawless. Whether OSRM succeeds or the offline fallback runs, the mechanic marker must *always* initialize precisely at `mechanicRoute.coordinates[0]`. I\'m double-checking the math in `/src/utils/routeAnimationMath.ts`, focusing on how `sampleRouteAtProgress` handles various cases. Specifically, I\'m verifying the edge cases where the coordinate list is empty or contains only a single point, to ensure a smooth and accurate marker start and route representation. The goal is to avoid any visual jitter, so that the mechanic overlay looks elegant.\n\n\n**Refining Animation Edge Cases**\n\nI\'m now refining the edge case logic, specifically focusing on the animation. If progress is complete, I\'ll set the current position to the final coordinate and empty the remaining route for a clean animation finish. If there\'s partial progress, I\'ll calculate the current position using the segment index and fraction, ensuring the remaining coordinates reflect the path ahead. This ensures a consistent and smooth overlay animation.\n\n\n**Calculating Remaining ETA**\n\nI\'m now focusing on calculating the remaining estimated time of arrival (ETA) precisely. I\'ve designed a helper function, `computeRemainingEtaMinutes`, which calculates the remaining ETA based on the initial ETA and the remaining fraction of the route. This function returns `0` if the remaining fraction is less than or equal to `0`; otherwise, it returns the ceiling of the product of the initial ETA and the remaining fraction, ensuring a minimum ETA of 1 minute.\n\n\n**Calculating Accurate ETA**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring that the estimated time of arrival (ETA) is accurate and synchronized across all visual elements. I\'ve developed the `computeRemainingEtaMinutes` function, which accurately calculates the remaining ETA based on the remaining fraction of the route. This ensures the MapLibre marker badge and the information card display the *exact* same, synchronized `liveEtaMinutes`.\n\n\n**Validating Edge Case Scenarios**\n\nI\'m verifying that all edge cases for the animation, starting position, and ETA calculations are thoroughly handled. I\'m making sure that the mechanic marker is always placed on the route, even when the data is sparse. The calculations need to reflect the full, final route with smooth updates for all visual components.\n\n\n**Refining State Management**\n\nI\'m now refining how the arrival and completion states are managed within the application, and have started looking into the necessary changes for proper handling. I\'m focusing on ensuring the visual cues and state transitions are seamless. My next step will be working on unit tests, which will begin when changes have been implemented.\n\n\n**Implementing Final Details**\n\nI\'m now implementing the final details to streamline the logic for arrival and completion, ensuring a smooth user experience. I\'ve integrated the route display updates and badge updates, ensuring that visual elements are synchronized and correct based on `trackingStatus`. When `progress >= 1`, the route is cleared and the badge is updated. When `trackingStatus` is `\'arrived\'` or `\'completed\'`, the route remains cleared and the marker is pinned at the final coordinate. I\'m focusing on minimizing updates for optimal performance.\n\n\n"
