  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Pinpointing Location Details**\n\nI just realized something significant! I reviewed screenshot `gas-pickup.png` and spotted \"S\341\273\237 N\303\264ng nghi\341\273\207p v\303\240 M\303\264i tr\306\260\341\273\235ng Th\303\240nh ph\341\273\221 H\341\273\223 Ch\303\255 Minh\" from Geoapify\'s reverse geocoding. This perfectly aligns with requirement #2 in the user\'s instructions. That\'s a critical connection to understanding their intent.\n\n\n**Refining Address Formatting**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing street address formatting from Geoapify reverse geocoding. I\'ve decided to avoid randomly grabbing nearby building names. Instead, I\'m forming address strings using a standardized order, prioritizing housenumber and street, followed by suburb, quarter, district, or city. This should ensure cleaner, more uniform address displays. I\'m also planning out all the requirements, trying to keep individual files at less than 250 lines.\n\n\n\n**Defining GPS Data Handling**\n\nI\'m currently focused on GPS data processing. I\'ve created `src/utils/gpsWatcher.ts` to manage GPS fixes. This new utility keeps the `useRescueGeo.ts` file small. It exports `GPS_WATCH_OPTIONS` as `PositionOptions` and also provides `pickBetterGpsSample()` to refine accuracy. I\'m building `watchBestGpsPosition()` to select the best GPS fix within a short time window.\n\n\n**Implementing GPS Refinement**\n\nI\'m now implementing GPS refinement in `src/hooks/useRescueGeo.ts`. I\'m working on `watchBestGpsPosition` with a timeout of 2500ms and a target accuracy of 20 meters. On geolocation success, I\'ll round the accuracy and update `activeLocation` immediately, running reverse geocoding for a clean address, while maintaining `accuracyMeters`. If GPS fails, I will use `DEFAULT_HCMC_COORD`, with `accuracyMeters: null`. I\'ll also expose `confirmPinnedLocation`.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Address Accuracy**\n\nI\'m now making address handling much cleaner. I\'m focusing on `src/utils/geocodeApi.ts`, refining `parseReverseGeocodeAddress`. I\342\200\231m ensuring I never use `props.name` or `props.address_line1`, as those contain agency names like \"S\341\273\237 N\303\264ng nghi\341\273\207p\". I\'m specifically building address parts from `housenumber`, `street`, `suburb`, `quarter`, `district`, or `city`, creating standardized address strings like the user\'s example: `\"182 \304\220i\341\273\207n Bi\303\252n Ph\341\273\247, Qu\341\272\255n B\303\254nh Th\341\272\241nh\"`. I\'m verifying proper formatting using existing examples.\n\n\n**Testing Address Refinement**\n\nI\'m updating `geocodeApi.test.ts` to fully validate address components, specifically `housenumber`, `street`, `quarter`, `suburb`, `district`, and `city`. I am ensuring that agency names are properly ignored. It\'s important to keep the full form of the district name, so I\'m updating my formatting logic to use `\"Qu\341\272\255n B\303\254nh Th\341\272\241nh\"` instead of a shorter form like `\"Q.1\"`. The test suite will verify the correct output.\n\n\n**Constructing Accuracy Circle**\n\nI\'m now focusing on drawing the GPS accuracy radius circle on the map. I\'ve decided to use a GeoJSON polygon with `fill` and `line` layers for this, directly scaled to real-world meters using the `accuracyMeters` value. I\'m building a helper function, `buildAccuracyCirclePolygon()`, in `src/utils/gpsWatcher.ts` to generate the circle\'s GeoJSON data, and I\'ll unit test it. The map\'s styling will be managed using blue color values from Tailwind.\n\n\n**Implementing Map Layer Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing map layer logic in `mapLayers.ts`. I\'m going to ensure the `\'gps-accuracy-circle\'` layer is below route lines. I\'ll update it with an accurate meter radius polygon when it\'s GPS data is current, and accuracy is valid. Otherwise, it will clear when not using GPS, guaranteeing a physically accurate radius at all zoom levels. I\'m focusing on the `gps-accuracy-stroke` layers.\n\n\n**Adding Map Pin Selection**\n\nI\'m now integrating an interactive map pin selection feature. I\'m adding a button with an orange pin icon to the UI, labeled \"Ch\341\273\215n tr\341\273\261c ti\341\272\277p tr\303\252n b\341\272\243n \304\221\341\273\223\". Clicking this will start map pin selection, and it will set `isPinPickerMode` to true, and initialize `pinnedPreview`. During the pin move I\'ll set `isResolving` to true. I also started working on `handleMapPinMoveEnd` to update `pinnedPreview`.\n\n\n\n**Finalizing Pin Selection**\n\nI\'m now integrating the final pieces for interactive map pin selection. I\'ve defined `handleMapPinMoveEnd` to manage pin movements. When the pin is moved, I clear previous geocoding, call `reverseGeocodeCoord`, and update `pinnedPreview`. On confirmation using `handleConfirmPinnedLocation`, I call the geo utility and re-center the map. I\'ve also implemented `handleCancelMapPinPicker` to cleanly exit the pin selection mode, and ensuring `isPinPickerMode` is false.\n\n\n**Designing Pin Picker UI**\n\nI\'m now designing the user interface for the map pin picker. When `isPinPickerMode` is true, I will render a fixed center pin overlay in the map viewport, with smooth visual effects. I will hide map filters to create more focus, and display a new bottom sheet (`<PinPickerSheet />`) containing a header badge, live address card, and cancel/confirm buttons. I\'ll maintain a live address display, and handle resolving with a subtle shimmer while geocoding, and show a full address result. Finally, I\'ll keep an eye on line count limits.\n\n\n**Refactoring Module Sizes**\n\nI\'m now focusing on file size management and modularity. I\'m proactively working to keep all files under 250 lines by extracting helper functions and components. I\'ve already updated `src/types/rescue.ts` and am revising `geocodeApi.ts` for clean address formatting. My focus is also on writing the tests for the new GPS tools. I need to continue to keep individual files at less than 250 lines.\n\n\n\n"
