  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing a New Hook**\n\nI\'m currently designing `src/hooks/useMapPinPicker.ts`. I aim to keep `useRescueOrderState.ts` below 250 lines and clean, so this is a crucial step. The new hook will be around 75 lines, primarily managing...\n\n\n**Defining Pin Picker State**\n\nI\'ve got the state variables for the pin picker hook sorted: `isPinPickerMode`, `isPinDragging`, `pinnedCoord`, `pinnedAddress`, and `isResolvingAddress`. I\'ve also defined the core functions: `startPinPicker`, `onPinMoveStart`, `onPinMoveEnd`, `cancelPinPicker`, and `confirmPinPicker`. I\'m integrating these into the map component, and updating the accuracy circle.\n\n\n**Integrating Pin Picker Mode**\n\nI\'m now integrating the pin picker mode into `RescueMap.tsx`. When active, I\'ll attach `movestart` and `moveend` listeners to the map. Also, I\'ll render the `CenterPinOverlay`, centered over the map. I\'ve noticed, however, the call to `easeTo({ center, padding })` is critical for ensuring the map\'s logical center matches the visual center after padding from the bottom sheet. I\'m double-checking alignment considering `getViewportPadding` and `CenterPinOverlay`. Specifically looking at how padding affects `map.getCenter()`.\n\n\n**Verifying Pin Alignment**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on precise pixel alignment. I\'ve realized the difference between the intended pin position (y=52 + (H - 52 - 240)/2) and the visual center (y = H / 2) is significant, about 94px! To fix this perfectly, I\'m going to compute the exact lat/lng at the visual pin screen point using `map.unproject([pinPixelX, pinPixelY])` within `onPinMoveEnd`. If the overlay places the pin\'s tip at `[el.clientWidth / 2, el.clientHeight * 0.36]`, this unprojects to the correct lat/lng. My plan is to verify this approach. I\'ll test it out!\n\n\n"
