    thinking: "**Analyzing User Requirements**\n\nI\'m meticulously dissecting the user\'s prompt, isolating each requirement to craft a modular solution. My goal is to design clean code with a line count under 250 per file, ensuring all features are preserved and every detail is addressed precisely.\n\n\n**Addressing Overlap Issue**\n\nI\'m focused on resolving the overlapping UI element issue in Step 2, specifically within `PickupSelectionStep.tsx`. The core problem lies with the height constraints of `BottomSheetShell` and the contents within it, leading to the `SafeStationListPicker` being hidden. To address this, I\'m analyzing the interplay of the scrollable container, the bottom pinned area, and the bottom sheet\'s maximum height, aiming to make all elements properly visible. My priority is \"Kh\341\272\257c ph\341\273\245c tri\341\273\207t \304\221\341\273\203 l\341\273\227i kh\341\273\221i\".\n\n\n**Refining UI Display**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the UI overlap fix, particularly in Step 2. My focus has shifted to ensuring the location list displays correctly above the total cost block. I\'m implementing a design with `max-h-[210px] overflow-y-auto space-y-2 pr-1` to enhance visibility and adding a bottom sheet collapse/expand button for users to view the map fully. Concurrently, I\'m integrating the display of phone numbers in Step 2\'s list, along with other UI locations. I\'m aiming for clarity in both the detail card and the homepage \"Open Now\" list.\n\n\n**Visualizing Map Markers**\n\nI\'m now determining the visual representation of map pins based on their type. I plan to use an emerald circle for repair shops that are open, a blue circle with a green dot for shops that are currently open, and an amber circle for the closest gas stations. Upon clicking a pin or selecting a location, I\'ll use the OSRM API to draw the route and automatically fit the bounds. Then, I\'ll present a floating card with place details and quick action buttons. Importantly, this will integrate with the existing `PickupSelectionStep`, which now lists different place types, not just safe stations.\n\n\n**Expanding Place Selection**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that users should have the option to wait at Open Repair Shops, Gas Stations, or Safe Stations in Step 2. I\'m updating the list in Step 2 to include Open Repair Shops, and, consistent with Requirement 3, displaying each shop\'s phone number alongside \"G\341\273\215i 0908...\" and \"\304\220\306\260\341\273\235ng \304\221i\" buttons. This integrates the map pins\' functionality with the detailed place lists, ensuring a unified user experience for route selection. I\'m focusing on \"Hi\341\273\203n th\341\273\213 r\303\265 s\341\273\221 \304\221i\341\273\207n tho\341\272\241i\" at this stage.\n\n\n**Integrating Location Details**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the direct integration of phone numbers and route options within Step 2\'s list. I\'m focusing on incorporating \"G\341\273\215i 0908...\" functionality and route drawing capabilities directly into the UI. I\'m ensuring consistent sorting across `SafeStation`, `openRepairShops`, and `gasStations`. The goal is to provide seamless selection and route integration, and I am also considering conversion of repair shops and gas stations to pickup points. The `rescueLogic.test.ts` will be checked later.\n\n\n**Implementing Map UI Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now integrating the expand/collapse button for the bottom sheet, as the map view should be fully visible, and I am making the map filter chips always visible. This addresses Requirement 1, ensuring a more comprehensive map display at every stage of the user flow. Specifically, I\'ve modified `MapTabView.tsx` to keep the filter chips visible regardless of the current step, enhancing the map view\'s overall usability. The plan is to continue this effort with `pricingAndSimLogic.test.ts` and `rescueLogic.test.ts`.\n\n\n**Enhancing Map Visibility**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring consistent visibility of map filter chips and adding a collapse/expand toggle button to the bottom sheet. My current thought is that rendering `<MapFilterChips />` should always happen if `!props.pinPicker.isActive`. The collapse/expand button, identified by `data-testid=\"toggle-sheet-btn\"`, will reside at the top handle bar. It will toggle between \"Thu g\341\273\215n b\341\272\243n \304\221\341\273\223\" / \"M\341\273\237 r\341\273\231ng\" with `ChevronDown` / `ChevronUp` icons. I also plan to automatically reset `isCollapsed` to `false` when the step changes to ensure a clear sheet opening.\n\n\n**Optimizing UI Placement**\n\nI\'m now refining the layout of `PickupSelectionStep` to maximize clarity and immediate visibility of key elements. The mode selection is compacted into a two-column grid, making it immediately visible. I\'ve ensured the place list\'s height constraint and overflow settings are clearly implemented, positioning the list of nearby locations (Safe Stations, Repair Shops, and Gas Stations) prominently above the pinned bottom invoice bar. I\'ve also incorporated the call and route options for each repair shop. My goal is to streamline the UI.\n\n\n**Clarifying UI Behavior**\n\nI\'m now determining the behavior of the nearby place list in Step 2. Should it stay visible or be hidden when \'Safe Station\' is selected? I\'m leaning towards keeping the list (with `max-h-[210px] overflow-y-auto space-y-2 pr-1`) visible in `safe_station` mode, even when in `current_location` mode, making navigation more intuitive and immediate. If a user clicks an item on the list, it will route via OSRM, setting the pickup target to that location.\n\n\n**Refining Interaction Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the interaction logic for place selection. If a user clicks a Safe Station in Step 2, `pickupMode` is set to \'safe_station\' and the route is drawn. If a Repair Shop is chosen, OSRM routes to that shop and optionally displays a Floating Place Card with route and call options. The nearby places list always stays visible above the invoice block, ensuring immediate visibility and the display of phone numbers alongside each repair shop in Step 2.\n\n\n**Adjusting Map Display**\n\nI\'m now focusing on refining the map\'s filter and display logic to ensure that gas stations are consistently visible in \"all\" mode. I\'m adjusting the code in `useMapMarkersSync.ts` to correctly show gas stations when `mapFilter` is \"all\" or `mapFilter` is \"gas_stations\". My goal is to rectify the oversight where gas stations were previously hidden in \"all\" mode, thereby improving the map\'s comprehensive representation. I will also make sure shops + gas stations show correctly when `step === \'idle\'`.\n\n\n**Updating Map Marker Display**\n\nI\'m now revising the map marker colors to align with user specifications, focusing on `mapMarkers.ts`. I\'m updating `createStationMarkerElement` to render Safe Stations as emerald circles (`bg-emerald-500`), open repair shops as blue circles with a green dot, and the closest gas stations as amber circles. I am ensuring that the visual representation accurately reflects each location type, especially the solid emerald for Safe Stations, addressing the user\'s specific request for clearer visual distinction. I will also make changes to the highlighted pins to reflect this.\n\n\n**Refining Marker Overlap**\n\nI\'m now determining how to deduplicate the map pins. If a Safe Station is also a gas station within 40m, I can either filter out the overlapping `gas_station` safe stations, or show all stations sorted by distance. In \'all\' mode, I want to show convenience stores, police stations, gas stations, and repair shops, but I\'ll need to prevent overlapping pins. I\'m focusing on clarifying marker display when `step` is idle, issue, or pickup.\n\n\n**Prioritizing Pin Differentiation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a combined approach for marker deduplication and display. My aim is to render all markers when `mapFilter` is \'all\' (and `step` isn\'t \'tracking\'). I\'ll keep the map clean during \'tracking\' by focusing on mechanic, user, and selected pickup station. I\'m prioritizing showing the top 5 nearest `stations`, top nearest `openRepairShops`, and all `stations` with duplicate pin overlap avoided. I\'ll utilize tests to verify marker IDs. I\'m planning to use OSRM API on pin clicks to draw routes.\n\n\n**Unifying Place Interaction**\n\nI\'m now consolidating how users interact with places (Safe Stations, Repair Shops, Gas Stations) through a unified `inspectedPlace` state. Whether a place is tapped on the map or selected from a list, `inspectedPlace` will be set to the corresponding place data. I\'m leveraging a discriminated union, `InspectedMapPlace`, to simplify the type handling for `safe_station`, `repair_shop`, and `gas_station`. Selecting a place will trigger OSRM API calls to draw precise routes, thus unifying the inspection/routing action.\n\n\n**Integrating Place Interaction**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a unified interaction pattern. Tapping a location, whether a safe station, repair shop, or gas station, sets the `inspectedPlace` state. This triggers an OSRM route, accurately fits the map bounds and updates the route summary. A floating place card is then displayed, providing route details and actions. This design ensures consistent user experience across different place types. I\'m focusing on \"Th\341\273\221ng nh\341\272\245t t\306\260\306\241ng t\303\241c\".\n\n\n**Refining UI Positioning**\n\nI\'m now streamlining the placement of map controls to ensure a non-overlapping and visually clean user interface. The `LocateFixed` button, crucial for recentering the map, will be placed directly above the `FloatingPlaceCard` with absolute positioning relative to `BottomSheetShell`. This will ensure it never overlaps. I\'m focusing on ensuring all UI elements work seamlessly together regardless of bottom sheet state.\n\n\n**Evaluating UI Consistency**\n\nI\'m now carefully evaluating the display consistency across different place types. I need to make sure the interactions with Repair Shops, Safe Stations, and Gas Stations are streamlined, especially in `idle` mode. Should the `RepairShopDetailSheet` open, or will `FloatingPlaceCard` directly above the Bottom Sheet provide a better user experience, with OSRM routing automatically on tap?\n\n\n**Evaluating Place Interaction**\n\nI\'m now focusing on standardizing the user experience across all place types (repair shops, safe stations, and gas stations). If I\'m in idle mode and a user clicks a repair shop pin, I need to ensure an OSRM route is drawn, the map adjusts to fit the bounds, and the `FloatingPlaceCard` displays with relevant information. I\'m prioritizing the `FloatingPlaceCard` as the main detail view to avoid redundant sheets and maintain consistency across steps, while ensuring the home step remains unchanged.\n\n\n**Standardizing Place Display**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the direct display of the `FloatingPlaceCard` above the bottom sheet for all place types, unifying the user experience. Whether selecting from the map or a list, tapping a place triggers route generation and the card appears, showing name, distance, time, and phone (for repair shops), along with call and route options. This approach simplifies the UI and integrates the phone number display seamlessly.\n\n\n**Unifying Place Interactions**\n\nI\'m now implementing the standardized behavior for all place interactions. I will ensure tapping any shop, station, or gas station pin in \'idle\' mode draws an OSRM route, adjusts the map bounds, and presents the `FloatingPlaceCard` with relevant details, including route options and contact information. In the home step, I am maintaining the existing behavior. I want to make sure I am unifying interactions across the user journey.\n\n\n**Standardizing Phone Handling**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing the accurate and standardized handling of phone numbers for repair shops. I\'m implementing a `resolveRepairShopPhone` function to handle missing phone data by assigning a curated hotline number based on the repair shop\'s ID, if no other phone data is available. This ensures that every repair shop has a valid phone number displayed in the `FloatingPlaceCard`, especially in idle mode.\n\n\n**Standardizing Phone Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating robust phone number display for repair shops, focusing on `FloatingPlaceCard.tsx` and the Step 2 list. I\'m implementing a `resolveRepairShopPhone` function to normalize existing phone data or generate a standardized Vietnamese mobile number (e.g., `09xx xxx xxx`) from the `shopId` if a phone is missing. This will be consistently displayed alongside \"G\341\273\215i\" and \"\304\220\306\260\341\273\235ng \304\221i\" buttons, ensuring uniform contact options and seamless route initiation, and I plan to test it by generating a deterministic hash by `shopId`.\n\n\n\n**Refining Place Display**\n\nI\'m now implementing a unified place list within Step 2, sorted by distance and filtered by map filter. The goal is a clear, concise display of nearby places, incorporating open repair shops with phone numbers, safe stations, and gas stations within the `max-h-[210px] overflow-y-auto space-y-2 pr-1` list. I\'m leveraging `MapFilterChips` for dynamic filtering.\n\n\n**Unifying Place Listing**\n\nI\'m now integrating a unified list in Step 2, sorting places by distance and applying map filters. This will display open repair shops with phone numbers, safe stations, and gas stations within the list, along with consistent filter integration. The OSRM route is activated on place selection.\n\n\n\n**Optimizing Card Interactions**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the card\'s action controls. My plan is to make sure `\"G\341\273\215i 0908...\"` correctly initiates a phone call directly, using `tel:...` and `e.stopPropagation()`. I\'ll employ a non-nested `<div role=\"article\">` card with distinct `<a href=\"tel:...\">` and `<button type=\"button\">\304\220\306\260\341\273\235ng \304\221i</button>` elements, ensuring clean HTML structure and avoiding nesting issues. I\'m also ensuring file budgets are maintained.\n\n\n**Testing Phone & Pin Refinement**\n\nI\'m working on tests for the `resolveRepairShopPhone` function, focusing on explicit phone priority, deterministic fallbacks, and valid Vietnamese mobile format. I\'m also ensuring Safe Station pins use emerald circles and updating map marker styling. Furthermore, I\'ve created the `FloatingPlaceCard` and integrated a collapse/expand toggle on the bottom sheet.\n\n\n**Integrating Place Interaction**\n\nNow, I\'m integrating unified place interaction logic, centralizing place inspection within `useActivePlaceInspect.ts`. Whether tapping map pins or selecting from lists, the `inspectedPlace` state is set, triggering OSRM routing and automatically fitting map bounds. This hook cleanly manages routes for safe stations, repair shops, and gas stations, enabling route summaries. My goal is streamlined interactions.\n\n\n**Unifying Route Management**\n\nI\'m now centralizing route management within `useActivePlaceInspect.ts` to streamline interactions. I\'m focusing on unifying interactions whether clicking map pins or selecting list entries.  My goal is to have the `inspectedPlace` trigger OSRM routing, automatically adjust map bounds, and ensure a smooth route summary, consistently.\n\n\n**Centralizing Route Logic**\n\nI\'m now working on centralizing all route-related functionality within the `useActivePlaceInspect.ts` hook. My focus is ensuring route calculations, map updates, and place interactions are consistently managed from a single source. This streamlined approach guarantees unified behavior for selecting places from lists or tapping map pins, leading to a smooth, intuitive user experience.\n\n\n**Streamlining Route Display**\n\nI\'m now integrating the route display based on place kind by leveraging existing map layers. My approach involves using `walking-route`, `shop-route`, and `gas-route` from `mapLayers.ts`, feeding them `inspectedRoute` based on `inspectedPlace.kind` and making sure that we display either `walkingRoute` or `inspectedRoute` based on current `step` and `pickupMode`. I\'m verifying this clean solution.\n\n\n**Refining Route Visualization**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring that the correct OSRM route layer (`shop-route`, `gas-route`, `walking-route`) is displayed based on the `inspectedPlace` kind and the current step, clearing the routes appropriately. I\'ve defined `InspectedPlace` type, and added `resolveRepairShopPhone` function. The `fitMapBoundsSafely` function is now run on the `inspectedRoute.coordinates` whenever it is non-null and `step` is not \'tracking\'.\n\n\n"
