    thinking: "**Designing Clean Architecture**\n\nOkay, I\'m setting up this architecture meticulously. My focus is on achieving a modular structure, keeping each file concise, ideally under 250 lines. The key is to address every aspect of the prompt and ensure the architecture is clean and robust, with a solid foundation. This is the initial framework for a really efficient system.\n\n\n**Defining Notification Types**\n\nI\'ve been working on the notification structure. I\'m focusing on clearly defining notification types using a TypeScript enum, incorporating crucial details like titles, messages, and timestamps. I\'m also including test cases to make sure everything works smoothly. The goal is to provide a clean and expandable foundation for handling notifications within the application.\n\n\n**Refining Notification Filters**\n\nI\'m now refining the notification filter logic. I\'ve designed helper functions in `notificationLogic.ts` that have unit tests. The functions include filtering by category (all, rescue, promo, safety), counting unread notifications, marking as read, and creating notification records. There\'s even a new function for formatting time labels as either `HH:mm` or \"V\341\273\253a xong.\" Plus, a tested audio chime with the Web Audio API.\n\n\n**Implementing Audio Cues**\n\nI\'m currently integrating audio feedback for notifications. I\'ve chosen two frequencies, approximately 660Hz and 880Hz, with a gentle exponential gain envelope. I\'m also thinking about where to implement this sound with an audio context.\n\n\n**Integrating Notification Logic**\n\nI\'m now integrating the notification logic into the application workflow. I\'m adding new unread notifications, triggering the chime, updating the banner, and handling push notifications. There are functions to mark as read, mark all as read, and dismiss the banner. I\'m also ensuring the implementation of the `useNotifications()` hook.\n\n\n**Designing Notification Interface**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the visual presentation and interactions of the notifications. I\'m implementing a slide-down animation with `motion/react` for entry and exit, handling swipe-up dismissal and click-to-open functionality. I\'ve got type-colored icon badges planned. I\'m also planning the modal design with a slide-up and fade-in effect, which includes the header and a toggle card for push notifications.\n\n\n**Visualizing Bell Integration**\n\nI\'m now focusing on integrating the bell button and notification list display. I\'m working on the design for the notification bell button within the `HomeTab.tsx` header, including visual cues for unread notifications. It will be placed next to `\"Test th\341\273\243\"`, with a frosted glass effect and a red unread badge. The modal design includes filter tabs, a \"Mark all as read\" button, and a scrollable list with visual distinctions between read and unread items. Clicking notifications should trigger the right navigation. I\'m also implementing the appropriate error messaging if the browser blocks notification permissions. I\'m currently working to make sure there are no duplicate messages.\n\n\n**Defining Notification Triggers**\n\nI\'m now implementing specific notification triggers. I\'m carefully defining each trigger event with its required data, especially regarding titles, messages, and target tabs. The goal is to provide timely and informative alerts. For example, for a successful rescue order, I\'ll prompt permission, then display the notification. Also, the th\341\273\243 acceptance triggers a specific message. And, one more time at 75% for the eta. All are designed inside specific component files.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Arrival Triggers**\n\nI\'m now concentrating on those final notification triggers, specifically when the mechanic gets close or arrives. I\'m carefully implementing the \"approaching\" notification with a targeted message when progress hits the 75% mark, and the mechanic is within the last 200m. I\'ve also implemented arrival notifications, confirming the mechanic\'s presence at the pick-up location. I\'m ensuring there\'s a seamless transition and timely information for the user. I\'m also preparing to integrate chat notifications, but need to find the correct call back to pass to the function.\n\n\n**Defining Chat Notifications**\n\nI\'m now implementing chat notification triggers. When the mechanic sends a message within the auto-reply timeout, I\'m invoking `onMechanicMessage`. This triggers a chat notification with the title \"Tin nh\341\272\257n m\341\273\233i t\341\273\253 Th\341\273\243 Tu\341\272\245n,\" the message content, the \'chat\' type, and the map target tab. I\'ve focused on ensuring this callback is properly integrated.\n\n\n"
